tools/lint — checks this repo runs on itself

Two linters, both stdlib-only, both wired into CI. Neither is about the game:
they are about what this repository writes down, in Lua and in git.


lua_comments.py — the comment budget

    lua_comments.py                 comment lines your branch adds
    lua_comments.py --base <ref>    pick the base ref explicitly
    lua_comments.py --all           every file (expect legacy findings)
    lua_comments.py --files a.lua   just these files, whole file

Caps a comment block at 8 lines and a file header at 30. The rule itself lives
in skills/ellesmereui-pr-check/scripts/check_style.py and is imported from
there rather than copied, so the addon gate and this one cannot disagree.

Diff-scoped by default: only the comment lines a change adds count toward the
budget. Editing one word inside a 40-line legacy block reports nothing. Without
that, every touch of an old file would open with a demand to rewrite comments
the change never made.


ascii_text.py — ASCII in commit messages and pull request text

    ascii_text.py --commit-msg .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG   one message, from a file
    ascii_text.py --range main..HEAD                 every message in a range
    ascii_text.py --stdin --label "PR body"          text on standard input
    ascii_text.py --command 'gh pr create -b "..."'  a shell command line
    ascii_text.py --hook-json                        a PreToolUse event, on stdin
    ascii_text.py --install-hook [repo]              install the commit-msg hook

Source files get linted, reviewed, and fixed. This text does not. It goes
straight into a place where it is quoted, re-encoded, and read back for years
by terminals, mail gateways, and changelog tooling that do not agree on an
encoding, and by the time a curly quote surfaces as mojibake the commit is
immutable.

Three call sites, one rule:

    commit-msg hook   every commit, per clone
                      python -m wow_tools install --hooks ascii-git-text --repo .
    PreToolUse hook   gh pr create / comment / review, before the call goes out
                      wired up in .claude/settings.json
    CI                the commit range, plus the PR title and body

The command modes are the interesting half. A pull request body never touches
git, so the only place to catch it is the command about to create it. Rather
than scanning the whole command line, --command and --hook-json pull out only
the parts that are prose: the values of -m, --message, --title, --body,
--notes, and any heredoc. A non-ASCII character in a path, a branch name, or an
unrelated command is ignored. That precision is the point -- a gate that fires
on filenames gets switched off within a day.

--body-file and -F are deliberately not read. They name a file, and a file on
disk gets linted as a file.

Exit status is 1 when anything is non-ASCII and 2 when the arguments are wrong,
except in --hook-json, where 2 is what PreToolUse reads as "block this call".

The replacement table is duplicated from check_style.py rather than imported,
because this script runs as a git hook inside a *different* repository and a
hook that fails because another checkout moved would block every commit there.
tests/test_ascii_text.py asserts the two tables agree wherever they overlap, so
the duplication cannot drift in silence.
